From f713d2eb5eccf69cf6db7d20221d9f95714c046e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 14:50:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] Believe 15.6.1, 14.6.1 and 13.6 in the wire watch, each
 by its layout

The watch and guard believed 27.2 and 26.6.2 only. The IPSW
kernelcaches of the other three (~/forensics/layouts-old.txt on dgx)
give two layouts:

- 15.6.1 (24G90) runs 27.2's driver at every offset read here: the
  0x98-byte MappingInternal with the surface descriptor at +0x20,
  +0x48/+0x50 zero, the page-table descriptor at +0x58 and the page
  count at +0x80.
- 13.6 (22G120) and 14.6.1 (23G93) keep the older 0x78-byte one: +0x20
  is the IOSurface, not a descriptor; the surface descriptor is the one
  IOSurface::getMemoryDescriptor returns (+0x38 on 13.6, +0x30 on
  14.6.1); the page-table descriptor is at +0x48, its mapping at +0x50,
  the page count at +0x70. +0x58 holds the eviction deadline once the
  mapping was completed and +0x60 is 1 when the table spans two pages,
  so neither is a reserved zero.

The IOMemoryDescriptor offsets are the same in all five.

KNOWN_KERNELS pairs each exact version string with its layout. The
string alone picks it, and a mapping that fails its kernel's layout is
not tried against the other. On the unshifted layout the IOSurface must
be one by its vtable: its address point lies a fixed distance above the
kernel's Mach-O header, 0xa5ddc8 on 13.6 (__ZTV9IOSurface
0xfffffe0007a65db8 + 0x10, header 0xfffffe0007008000) and 0xb0fde8 on
14.6.1 (0xfffffe0007b17dd8 + 0x10). Those two were not in layouts-old
or its excerpts; they were read for this from kernelcaches re-extracted
from the IPSWs and deleted afterwards, and IOSurface's constructor
stores exactly that address, signed. That the distance holds at run
time rests on the boot kernel collection being slid as one unit (its
chained fixups all count from one base); no 13.6 or 14.6.1 object was
read. A mismatch is refused as iosurface_class with the distance seen.
The surface descriptor's class is checked as before: the page-table
descriptor's, inside the kernel's __DATA_CONST. Every layout now also
checks MappingInternal +0x18 against the mapper device, as the resolve
does; the +0x48/+0x50 zeros are checked on the shifted layout only.

The chain, MappingInternal to IOSurface to both descriptors, is read
twice back to back at every check and every sample, and is used only
when both reads name the same objects (all but the counts, the driver's
state word and the mixed data). When they differ nothing is concluded:
OFF wire_watch inconclusive mapping= link= n= at=, counted, read again
later. A failed read concludes nothing either, as before. A sample whose
chain differs from the checked one is retired, as an id change was; it
now covers the IOSurface and the descriptors' class and fields too. None
of these is ever read as a count or condemns.

Logs: wire_watch_kernel verified and layout_unverified scope=mapping
name macos= and layout=; wire_watch map gains iosurface=; new mapping
reasons mi_owner, iosurface_pointer, iosurface_class; the cost and unmap
lines gain inconclusive=. On 26.6.2 and 27.2 the layout and checks are
unchanged but for the owner check and the second read, and a descriptor
reallocated under a checked mapping is now retired rather than read.

Tests (runtime::mapper::wire_watch, --test-threads=1): 32 passed, 0
failed; 5 new: 15.6.1 believed and guarded with the shifted layout;
13.6 and 14.6.1 read through their IOSurface, a decoy at 13.6's +0x30
passed over, surface and table drops found; non-zero and moving
+0x58/+0x60 on the unshifted layout pass and move nothing; seven
mappings refused by name against their own kernel's layout only; and
an object swapped between the two reads and back (13.6, 14.6.1, 27.2)
is inconclusive, never a count, before a real drop is found. The 26.6.2
test's mismatch loop now uses near misses of the known strings, and the
layout breakages add mi_unshifted_layout and mi_owner. No guest was
booted.

